Moonlight: isolate VA-API presentation and keep only the newest frame (#466)

vaPutSurface waits for X Present while holding Mesa's VA display mutex.
With decode on that display, CAPABILITY_DIRECT_SUBMIT stalls UDP reception
behind vblank. The backlog grows in the socket, not the X11 pointer pipe.
SDL instead keeps decoding while its presentation thread waits for vblank.

Use an independent X11-backed VA display for presentation, and share decoded
surfaces through DMA-buf. Cache imports for the lifetime of their frame pool.
The X11 hand-off retains exactly one pending frame and owns every frame it
presents, independent of the decoder's reusable AVFrame wrappers.

VA's export contract requires synchronization before external reads; keep
that synchronization and the decoded frame alive until rendering completes.
Cost: a second VA display and roughly 1.5 ms of decode synchronization on aka.
No FFmpeg rebuild, renderer change, driver-specific toggle or new dependency.

